const body = String.raw`
<div class="tldr-box">
	<h2>TL;DR</h2>
	<p>
		On <span class="text-tt">OGBench Ant-Maze</span> we train a
		<b>VLM-based VLA</b> (Qwen3-VL 2B + LoRA on ViT and the LLM head, OpenVLA-OFT
		style) in two settings:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<b>Top-view (MDP) — coordinate VQA.</b> Given the bird's-eye image plus
			<span class="text-tt">Go to (x, y)</span>, the VLM emits a sequence of
			<b>intermediate trajectory waypoint coordinates</b>
			$(x_1, y_1), \ldots, (x_K, y_K)$. A small low-level controller turns
			waypoints into 8-DOF torques.
		</li>
		<li>
			<b>3rd-person (POMDP) — direct action VLA.</b> Same backbone, same
			recipe, but the head decodes the action chunk directly from the partial
			3rd-person observation.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		<b>Both settings mean-collapse</b>, and the <b>top-view (MDP) collapse is the
		more severe one</b>. In the bare top-view + direct-action setting success is
		<b>0%</b>; only the coordinate-VQA reformulation makes top-view work at all.
		Beyond classical state-aliasing, the cause specific to VLM-based VLA is
		<b>ViT-level feature aliasing</b>: the ant covers only a few pixels in
		top-view, ViT patch tokens are nearly constant across the episode, and the
		LLM ends up averaging over many states. Higher-resolution top-view input
		recovers performance — confirming the diagnosis.
	</p>
	<p>
		A small <b>memory-based steering module</b> (GLA Linear-SSM + Perceiver,
		injected into the <b>frozen</b> VLA) <b>solves mean collapse</b> on the
		3rd-person POMDP, lifting the success rate above the top-view MDP baseline.
	</p>
	<p>
		The same Markovian-on-$o_t$ pathology shows up beyond robotics — a real GUI
		agent (<a href="https://github.com/stepfun-ai/gelab-zero">GELab-Zero-4B-preview</a>)
		gets stuck in a <span class="text-tt">C → D → E → C → D → E → …</span> loop
		on a Duolingo &ldquo;tap the matching pairs&rdquo; quiz.
	</p>
</div>

<hr />
<h1>1. MDP vs. POMDP — and Why Mean Collapse Happens</h1>

<p>
	A <b>Markov Decision Process</b> $\mathcal{M} = (\mathcal{S}, \mathcal{A}, P, R, \gamma)$
	gives the agent the underlying state $s_t$ at every step:
</p>

<p>$$\pi^{\star}_{\mathrm{MDP}}(a_t \mid s_t).$$</p>

<p>
	A <b>Partially Observable MDP</b>
	$\mathcal{P} = (\mathcal{S}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{O}, P, R, \Omega, \gamma)$
	hides the state behind an observation kernel
	$o_t \sim \Omega(\cdot \mid s_t)$. The optimal policy must condition on history
	(or on a belief $b_t$):
</p>

<p>
	$$
	\pi^{\star}_{\mathrm{POMDP}}(a_t \mid h_t),\qquad
	h_t = (o_0, a_0, \ldots, o_t).
	$$
</p>

<div class="block-statement">
	A Markovian policy on $o_t$ is suboptimal whenever $o_t \not\Rightarrow s_t$.
</div>

<h2>1.1 Why Mean Collapse Happens</h2>

<p>
	A VLM-based VLA trained to act on the latest observation only learns a
	Markovian policy $\pi(a_t \mid o_t)$. When several distinct underlying states
	alias to similar observations
	($s^{(1)} \neq s^{(2)} \neq \cdots$ but
	$o^{(1)} \approx o^{(2)} \approx o$), the loss is minimized by the
	<b>posterior-weighted average</b> of the per-state optimal actions:
</p>

<p>
	$$
	\pi^{\star}(a \mid o)
	\;=\; \mathbb{E}_{s \sim p(s \mid o)}\!\left[\pi^{\star}(a \mid s)\right]
	\;=\; \sum_{i} p\!\left(s^{(i)} \mid o\right)\, \pi^{\star}\!\left(a \mid s^{(i)}\right).
	$$
</p>

<p>
	When the optimal $\pi^{\star}(a \mid s^{(i)})$ for different $s^{(i)}$ point in
	<b>opposite directions</b>, this average is a <b>degenerate output</b>: a
	near-zero motion, a sideways drift, an oscillation. That is <b>mean collapse</b>.
	On Ant-Maze it appears <b>even on the privileged top-view (MDP) input</b> — for
	a reason that is specific to VLM-based VLAs and that we dissect in Section 6.
</p>

<hr />
<h1>2. Most VLA Benchmarks Hide the POMDP</h1>

<p>
	Most VLA benchmarks are effectively MDPs: the current scene already encodes the
	task-relevant state, so a policy reading only the latest observation looks fine
	(<span class="text-tt">LIBERO</span>, <span class="text-tt">SIMPLER</span>).
	GUI agents (<span class="text-tt">UI-TARS</span>-style) decide each action from
	a <b>summary plus the current screenshot only</b>. Both setups assume
	$\pi(a_t \mid o_t)$ suffices.
</p>
<p>
	But real long-horizon tasks (Minecraft, GUI agents, manipulation under
	occlusion) need <b>history</b> $h_t$, not just $o_t$. Crafting a pickaxe needs
	the fact &ldquo;I already chopped a tree&rdquo; — which is not in the current
	screen.
</p>

<hr />
<h1>3. A Controllable POMDP in OGBench Ant-Maze</h1>

<p>
	We use the public <a href="https://github.com/seohongpark/ogbench">OGBench</a>
	Ant-Maze tasks and re-render them in MuJoCo with two cameras:
</p>

<ul>
	<li><b>Top-view (MDP).</b> $o_t = s_t$ — full position, goal, and maze layout in one bird's-eye image.</li>
	<li><b>3rd-person (POMDP).</b> $o_t \sim \Omega(\cdot \mid s_t)$ — only what is in front of the ant.</li>
</ul>

<div class="figure">
	<img src="${fig('two_views.png')}" alt="Same world, two views" />
	<span class="caption">
		Same world, two views. <b>Left:</b> top-view (MDP). <b>Right:</b> 3rd-person
		(POMDP). The two settings differ only in observability.
	</span>
</div>

<hr />
<h1>4. Setup</h1>

<p>
	Concrete numbers, from the
	<a href="https://github.com/MK040412/Weasel_toy_experiment">Weasel Toy Experiment</a>
	repository.
</p>

<h2>4.1 Data — AntMaze paired dataset</h2>
<ul>
	<li>Source: OGBench <code>antmaze-{medium,large,giant}-navigate-v0</code>, re-rendered through MuJoCo (<span class="text-tt">osmesa</span>).</li>
	<li>$3$ mazes $\times$ $1{,}000$ episodes $\times \sim 1{,}001$ steps, $224 \times 224 \times 3$ uint8, two cameras (<span class="text-tt">obs_topdown</span>, <span class="text-tt">obs_third</span>).</li>
	<li>Underlying control: $8$-DOF joint torque (OGBench native); <span class="text-tt">qpos</span>: $15$-D, <span class="text-tt">qvel</span>: $14$-D.</li>
	<li>Hindsight relabeling: chunk $K = 16$, terminal-goal fraction $0.25$.</li>
</ul>

<h2>4.2 Two policy heads, one backbone</h2>

<p>
	The same backbone — <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.21631">Qwen3-VL 2B</a>,
	fine-tuned in the <a href="https://openvla-oft.github.io/">OpenVLA-OFT</a> style
	(LoRA on ViT and the LLM head, parallel chunk decode) — is used in two settings
	with different output heads:
</p>

<ul>
	<li>
		<b>Top-view (MDP) — coordinate VQA.</b> Input: top-view image $o_t^{\,\text{top}}$
		plus <span class="text-tt">Go to (x, y)</span>. Output: a sequence of
		intermediate trajectory waypoint coordinates
		$(x_1, y_1), (x_2, y_2), \ldots, (x_K, y_K)$ as text tokens. A small
		low-level controller turns waypoints into 8-DOF torques.
	</li>
	<li>
		<b>3rd-person (POMDP) — direct action VLA.</b> Input: 3rd-person image
		$o_t^{\,\text{3rd}}$ plus the same instruction. Output: a parallel-decoded
		<b>action chunk</b> directly in joint-torque space (OpenVLA-OFT default).
	</li>
</ul>

<div class="figure">
	<img src="${fig('ant_vla_training.png')}" alt="Ant VLA training pipeline" />
	<span class="caption">
		One backbone (Qwen3-VL 2B + LoRA on ViT and LLM head), two output heads
		(top-view coordinate-VQA waypoints; 3rd-person direct action chunk).
	</span>
</div>

<div class="block-statement">
	The only thing that differs between the two settings is observability and the
	output head — same backbone, same data, same training recipe.
</div>

<hr />
<h1>5. Both Views Mean-Collapse — Bare Top-View Collapses to 0%</h1>

<p>
	Naively the <b>top-view (MDP)</b> agent should be a clean upper bound — it sees
	the underlying state, after all. In practice, the <b>direct-action</b>
	top-view VLA exhibits the <b>most severe mean collapse</b>: the predicted
	action sequence collapses toward near-zero displacement and the rollout is
	degenerate — the ant walks in place, drifts sideways, tips over, or simply
	gets marked as failed and halts. The trick that rescues top-view is the
	<b>coordinate-VQA reformulation</b> from Section 4.2 — predicting waypoint
	coordinates as text instead of low-level actions — which lifts the top-view
	success rate to the value reported in the original presentation. We summarize
	all four settings in the results table at the end of Section 7.
</p>

<p>
	The <b>3rd-person (POMDP)</b> rollouts also mean-collapse, but the failure is
	<b>less degenerate in motion</b>: the agent actually walks around, explores
	corridors, and occasionally — when the pink goal happens to come into view —
	turns and dives into it. These are <b>opportunistic</b> wins, not principled
	ones.
</p>

<h2>5.1 Top-view (MDP) — different loop-case examples</h2>

<p>
	Each video below is a <b>different example of the same loop / mean-collapse
	pathology</b> on the top-view input.
</p>

<div class="grid-2">
	<div class="figure">
		<video controls muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" src="${vid('fail_falling.mp4')}"></video>
		<span class="caption-muted">
			Loop-case example 1 — the ant tips over almost immediately at the start.
			The simulation marks the episode as failed and the rollout simply halts in
			place for the rest of the run. (Full video shown.)
		</span>
	</div>
	<div class="figure">
		<video controls muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" src="${vid('fail_loop.mp4')}"></video>
		<span class="caption-muted">
			Loop-case example 2 — the agent twitches in place and cycles between the
			same two actions in a corridor.
		</span>
	</div>
</div>

<h2>5.2 Top-view — extreme stuck case</h2>

<div class="figure">
	<video controls muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" src="${vid('mean_collapse_after_40.mp4')}"></video>
	<span class="caption-muted">
		Loop-case example 3 — after ~step 40 the predicted waypoints collapse to a
		near-zero mean and the ant is frozen in place for the rest of the rollout.
	</span>
</div>

<h2>5.3 3rd-person (POMDP) — opportunistic successes</h2>

<p>
	On the 3rd-person input the same Markovian recipe still mean-collapses, but
	the agent moves more — and sometimes wins by sheer exploration.
</p>

<div class="grid-2">
	<div class="figure">
		<video controls muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" src="${vid('success_00.mp4')}"></video>
		<span class="caption-muted">3rd-person, no memory — opportunistic success.</span>
	</div>
	<div class="figure">
		<video controls muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" src="${vid('success_03.mp4')}"></video>
		<span class="caption-muted">3rd-person, no memory — opportunistic success on a different seed.</span>
	</div>
</div>

<hr />
<h1>6. Why the Top-View Collapse is Worse — ViT-Level Feature Aliasing</h1>

<p>
	The classical mean-collapse story (Section 1.1) is about <b>state aliasing in
	the observation space</b>: two states map to similar pixel images. On the
	top-view that argument should not apply — the underlying state $s_t$ is fully
	visible. Yet the bare top-view collapses to 0%. Why?
</p>

<p>
	The aliasing happens <b>one level above the pixels</b>, in the
	<b>ViT feature space</b>:
</p>

<ul>
	<li>
		<b>Tiny scene-relevant region.</b> In the top-view, the ant and the pink goal
		together cover only a small fraction of the image. Each $14 \times 14$ ViT
		patch sees mostly empty maze floor; the ant occupies only a handful of
		pixels inside one or two patches.
	</li>
	<li>
		<b>Near-identical patch features across the episode.</b> Because the maze
		layout is the same and only a few pixels carry the state, the ViT patch
		tokens are <b>close to constant across timesteps</b>. From the LLM's point of
		view the input embedding barely moves — &ldquo;every scene looks the same.&rdquo;
	</li>
	<li>
		<b>Broad posterior</b> $p(s \mid \phi(o))$. With nearly constant ViT
		features $\phi(o)$, the posterior over underlying states given features is
		broad. The mean-collapse identity from Section 1.1 then forces the predicted
		output toward the average over many states — a near-zero action.
	</li>
	<li>
		<b>3rd-person is the opposite.</b> Scene-relevant content
		(walls in front, corridor turns, the pink goal when it appears) <b>fills the
		ViT patches</b>. The features change clearly between observations, the
		per-feature posterior is sharper, and there is enough signal to commit to a
		direction at each step.
	</li>
</ul>

<div class="block-statement">
	Top-view <b>has the state, but loses it inside the ViT</b>. 3rd-person is
	formally a POMDP, yet its features are sharper — so the Markovian-VLA failure
	mode is less violent.
</div>

<h2>6.1 Sanity check — higher-resolution top-view fixes it</h2>

<p>
	If ViT-level feature aliasing is really the cause, then giving the ViT more
	pixels per scene should sharpen the features and reduce collapse. We confirmed
	this directly: feeding <b>higher-resolution top-view images</b> into the ViT
	(at the same coordinate-VQA prompt) <b>recovers performance on the top-view
	task</b>. Same backbone, same training, same head — only the input resolution
	changes.
</p>

<p>
	Useful diagnostic: when a VLM-based VLA mean-collapses on a supposedly-MDP
	input, suspect <b>resolution / feature aliasing</b> before suspecting the
	policy class.
</p>

<hr />
<h1>7. The Fix — A Memory-Based Steering Module on the Frozen VLA</h1>

<div class="figure">
	<img src="${fig('steering_module.png')}" alt="Steering module added to the frozen VLA" />
	<span class="caption">
		Learnable steering (peach) builds a fixed-size memory $Z$ from past ViT
		features via a <b>GLA temporal mixer (Linear SSM)</b> + <b>Perceiver
		compression</b>, then injects it into the frozen ViT at L8 / L16 and into the
		action head through gated residuals. The frozen base VLA (green) is never
		re-trained.
	</span>
</div>

<div class="block-statement">
	The steering module is what <b>solves mean collapse</b> on the 3rd-person
	POMDP — recovering the success probability lost to partial observability and
	pushing past the top-view MDP baseline.
</div>

<h2>7.1 Formulation</h2>

<p>
	Past ViT features $h^{(\ell)}_{t-k}$ at depths $\ell \in \{8, 16\}$ for
	$k = 1, \ldots, K$ are mixed into a fixed-size state by a <b>Gated Linear
	Attention</b> (Linear SSM) with content-gated decay:
</p>

<p>
	$$
	\begin{aligned}
	S_k &= \sigma(g_k) \odot S_{k-1} + K_k^{\top} V_k, \\
	O_k &= Q_k\, S_k.
	\end{aligned}
	$$
</p>

<p>
	The mixed sequence $O^{(\ell)}_{1:K}$ is compressed to $m = 32$ memory tokens
	via a <b>Perceiver</b> cross-attention with learnable queries
	$Q_{\text{learn}} \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times d}$:
</p>

<p>
	$$
	Z^{(\ell)} \;=\; \mathrm{CrossAttn}\!\left(Q_{\text{learn}},\; O^{(\ell)}_{1:K}\right) \;\in\; \mathbb{R}^{m \times d}.
	$$
</p>

<p>
	The memory is <b>gated-residual injected</b> back into the frozen ViT at depths
	$\ell \in \{8, 16\}$:
</p>

<p>
	$$
	\tilde{h}^{(\ell)}_t \;=\; h^{(\ell)}_t \;+\; \sigma(\alpha_\ell)\,
	\mathrm{CrossAttn}\!\left(h^{(\ell)}_t,\; Z^{(\ell)}\right).
	$$
</p>

<p>And a small residual is added to the frozen action head:</p>

<p>
	$$
	a_t \;=\; \underbrace{\pi_{\text{base}}(o_t)}_{\text{frozen}}
	\;+\; \sigma(\alpha_{\text{res}})\, \Delta\!\left(Z^{(16)}\right).
	$$
</p>

<p>
	Identity-init gates ($\sigma(\alpha) = 0$) make the module a <b>no-op at
	initialization</b>, so it recovers the base VLA exactly and only learns
	corrections.
</p>

<h2>7.2 Why a State-Space Model?</h2>

<ul>
	<li><b>Long sequence.</b> Flattened past is $T = K \cdot N_{\text{patch}} \sim 2{,}000$ tokens. Quadratic attention is $\mathcal{O}(T^2)$; a Linear SSM is $\mathcal{O}(T)$ with a fixed-size state $S$.</li>
	<li><b>Content-gated decay.</b> $\sigma(g_t)$ is a per-token forget — more expressive than RetNet's fixed $\gamma$, more stable than plain linear attention.</li>
	<li><b>Length extrapolation.</b> Train with $K \sim \mathcal{U}[1, 32]$ and sinusoidal temporal PE; remains stable at $K \in \{64, 128\}$ at inference.</li>
	<li><b>Identity-init gates.</b> The module starts as a no-op, recovers the base VLA exactly, then learns corrections safely.</li>
</ul>

<h2>7.3 Results — full picture in one table</h2>

<p>
	With the cause and the fix in place, the four numbers tell the whole story.
	The bare top-view + direct-action setting mean-collapses to 0%. The
	coordinate-VQA reformulation (Section 4.2) lifts top-view to the presentation
	number. The 3rd-person direct-action setting suffers from partial
	observability. Adding the steering module (Section 7) on the 3rd-person input
	pushes success past the top-view MDP baseline — without re-training the base
	VLA.
</p>

