	</span>
</div>

<hr />
<h1>8. Bonus — Same Pathology on a GUI Agent</h1>

<p>
	The exact same Markovian-on-$o_t$ failure shows up in a real GUI agent.
	<a href="https://github.com/stepfun-ai/gelab-zero">GELab-Zero-4B-preview</a>, a
	VLM-based agent, runs on a Duolingo &ldquo;tap the matching pairs&rdquo; quiz.
	At every step it receives the current screenshot and emits Thought + Action.
</p>

<div class="figure">
	<img src="${fig('gui_agent_case.png')}" alt="Testing GELab-Zero on Duolingo matching quiz" />
	<span class="caption">
		<span class="text-tt">GELab-Zero-4B-preview</span> on a Duolingo matching
		quiz. At every step the agent only sees the current screenshot.
	</span>
</div>

<h2>8.1 The Loop-Case</h2>

<p>
	Solved pairs turn slightly darker, but solved and fresh tiles look almost
	identical — and the agent has no memory of which ones it already tapped. So
	it re-taps a solved pair, the app rejects it, the screen returns to the same
	state, and the agent enters a three-step loop:
</p>

<p>
	$$
	C \;\rightarrow\; D \;\rightarrow\; E \;\rightarrow\; C \;\rightarrow\; D \;\rightarrow\; E \;\rightarrow\; \ldots
	$$
</p>

<div class="figure">
	<img src="${fig('gui_loop_case.png')}" alt="GUI loop case — C → D → E → C → D → E …" />
	<span class="caption">
		Loop-case definition. Same pathology as the Ant POMDP: $o_t$ (screenshot)
		$\not\Rightarrow s_t$ (solved-set).
	</span>
</div>

<h2>8.2 GUI rollout — a separate loop-case example</h2>

<p>
	A different concrete instance of the same loop, captured live during a
	GELab-Zero rollout:
</p>

<div class="figure">
	<video controls muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" src="${vid('gui_rollout.mp4')}"></video>
	<span class="caption-muted">
		GUI agent rollout (sped up) — a separate loop-case example, distinct from
		the Ant videos. The agent enters the
		<span class="text-tt">C → D → E → C → D → E → …</span> cycle described above.
	</span>
</div>

<hr />
<h1>9. Takeaway</h1>

<ul>
	<li><b>Mean collapse is a major bottleneck for VLM-based VLAs.</b> A Markovian policy on $o_t$ is forced to output the posterior-weighted average of per-state optimal actions; when those disagree, the average is degenerate.</li>
	<li><b>Even the privileged top-view does not save you.</b> On Ant-Maze, the bare top-view (MDP) direct-action setting collapses to <b>0%</b>; only the coordinate-VQA reformulation rescues it.</li>
	<li><b>The cause one level above pixels is ViT-level feature aliasing.</b> When the scene-relevant content covers only a few pixels, patch tokens are nearly constant across timesteps; the LLM sees almost the same input every step. Higher-resolution top-view input fixes it.</li>
	<li><b>3rd-person is POMDP but feature-rich</b> — patches are filled with scene content, so the agent moves more and occasionally wins by exploration.</li>
	<li><b>A small memory-based steering module on the frozen VLA</b> (GLA Linear-SSM + Perceiver, identity-init gated injection) is the lightweight fix that <b>raises the 3rd-person success rate above the top-view MDP baseline</b> without re-training the base VLA.</li>
	<li><b>The same pathology shows up beyond robotics</b> — a frozen GUI agent on a Duolingo quiz enters the same <span class="text-tt">C → D → E → …</span> loop for the same reason.</li>
</ul>

